hadiths[249]{hadithnumber,text}:
"751","751/973- (Hasan al-Isnad) From Abd al-Rahman ibn Ruzayn who said: We passed by al-Rabadhah, and it was said to us: Here is Salamah ibn al-Akwa‘. So we came to him and greeted him with salam, and he extended his hands and said: With these two I pledged allegiance to the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. Then he extended a large palm, as if it were the palm of a camel, so we stood up to it and kissed it."
"752","752/977- (Sahih) From Abu Majliz who said: Muawiya came out while Abdullah ibn Amir and Abdullah ibn al-Zubair were sitting. Ibn Amir stood up, but Ibn al-Zubair remained seated—and he was the most dignified of them. Muawiya said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Whoever is pleased that the servants of Allah stand up for him, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"753","753/978-(Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Allah created Adam, peace be upon him, [ على صورته] (1), and his height was sixty cubits. Then He said: Go and greet those— a group of angels sitting— and listen to how they greet you, for it will be your greeting and the greeting of your descendants. So he said: Peace be upon you. They said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. So they added: and the mercy of Allah. So everyone who enters Paradise will be in his image. The creation has continued to decrease in size until now."
"754","754/979-(Hasan) From Al-Bara’, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Spread the greeting of peace and you will be safe."
"755","755/980- From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not tell you about something which, if you do it, you will love one another?"" They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""Spread peace among yourselves."
"756","756/981-(Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Amr who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Worship the Most Merciful, feed food, and spread peace, you will enter the gardens."
"757","757/982-(Authentic chain) From Bashir ibn Yasar who said: ""No one would begin—or hasten—to greet Ibn Umar with salam."
"758","758/983-(Authentic chain, Mawquf) From Jabir who said: ""The rider greets the one walking, the one walking greets the one sitting, and of two people walking, whichever of them begins with the greeting is better."
"759","759/984- (Hasan) From Ibn Umar: That Al-Agharr – and he was a man from Muzaynah, and he had companionship with the Prophet, peace be upon him – had some wasqs of dates owed to him by a man from Banu Amr ibn Awf. He went to him repeatedly. He said: So I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he sent Abu Bakr As-Siddiq with me. He said: Everyone we met greeted us with salam. Abu Bakr said: ""Do you not see that the people are initiating you with salam, so they get the reward? Initiate them with salam so you will have the reward."" Ibn Umar narrates this about himself."
"760","760/985-(Sahih) From Abu Ayyub; that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""It is not lawful for a Muslim man to shun his brother for more than three days, so they meet and this one turns away and that one turns away; and the better of the two is the one who initiates the greeting of peace."
"761","761/986- (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, that a man passed by the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, while he was in a gathering, and said: ""Peace be upon you."" He said: ""Ten good deeds."" Then another man passed by and said: ""Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah."" He said: ""Twenty good deeds."" Then another man passed by and said: ""Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings."" He said: ""Thirty good deeds."" Then a man from the gathering stood up and did not give salam! The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""How quickly your companion forgot! When one of you comes to a gathering, let him give salam, and if it appears to him to sit, let him sit, and if he stands up (and in another narration: if he sits and then it appears to him to stand before the gathering disperses/__QUR_N__), let him give salam; the first is not more deserving than the last."
"762","762/987- (Authentic chain of narration) From Umar who said: I was riding behind Abu Bakr, and he would pass by some people and say: Peace be upon you, and they would reply: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. And he would say: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, and they would reply: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. So Abu Bakr said: Today, the people have surpassed us with much increase."
"763","763/988- (Sahih) From Aisha, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""The Jews have not envied you for anything as much as they have envied you for the greeting of peace and saying 'Ameen'."
"764","764/989- (Hasan) From Anas who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, as-Salam is one of the names of Allah, the Exalted. He placed it on the earth, so spread salam among yourselves."
"765","765/990- (Sahih) From Ibn Mas'ud who said: They used to pray behind the Prophet, peace be upon him, and someone said: Peace be upon Allah. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, finished his prayer, he said: ""Who said: Peace be upon Allah? Indeed, Allah is As-Salam (the Source of Peace), but say: All greetings, prayers, and good things are for Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger."" He said: And they used to learn it as one of you learns a surah from the Qur'an."
"766","766/991- (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The rights of a Muslim over another Muslim are six."" It was said: And what are they [ يا رسول الله/925] ? He said: ""If you meet him, greet him with salam; if he invites you, respond to him; if he seeks your advice, advise him; if he sneezes and praises Allah, bless him; if he falls ill, visit him; and if he dies, accompany him (and in another narration: follow him)."
"767","767/992- (Sahih) From Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl who said: I heard the Prophet, peace be upon him, say: ""The rider should greet the walker, the walker should greet the one sitting, and the fewer should greet the more numerous. Whoever responds to the greeting, it is for him; and whoever does not respond, he gets nothing.""(4)."
"768","768/993- From Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""The rider greets the walker, (and in another narration: the younger greets the elder/1001), the walker greets the one who is sitting, and the few greet the many."
"769","769/996- (Sahih) From Fadala [ بن عبيد /998] , from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The rider greets the one who is sitting, (in one narration: 'the rider greets the one who is walking, and the one who is walking greets the one who is sitting, and in another: the one who is standing/999), and the few greet the many.'"
"770","770/997- (Authentic chain of narration) From Husayn, from al-Sha'bi: That he met a Persian, and he initiated the greeting of peace to him. I said: ""You initiate the greeting of peace to him?"" He said: ""I saw Shurayh walking and initiating the greeting of peace.""(1)."
"771","771/(…)- And Asma said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, gestured with his hand to the women in greeting."
"772","772/1004- From Ata ibn Abi Rabah who said: ""They used to dislike greeting with the hand,"" or he said: ""He used to dislike greeting with the hand.""(1)."
"773","773/1005- (Authentic chain of narration) From Thabit ibn Ubayd who said: I came to a gathering in which was Abdullah ibn Umar, and he said: ""When you give salam, then listen; for it is a greeting from Allah, blessed and good."
"774","774/1006- From Al-Tufayl ibn Abi ibn Ka'b: That he used to come to Abdullah ibn Umar, and would go with him to the market in the morning. He said: Whenever we went to the market, Abdullah ibn Umar would not pass by a seller of trinkets, nor a shopkeeper, nor a poor person, nor anyone except that he would greet him with peace. Al-Tufayl said: I came to Abdullah ibn Umar one day, and he asked me to accompany him to the market. I said: What do you do in the market? You do not stop at the sales, nor ask about the goods, nor bargain for them, nor sit in the gatherings of the market. So sit with us here and let us talk. Abdullah said to me: ""O Abu Batn! – and Al-Tufayl had a large belly – we only go out for the sake of greeting [نسلم] those whom we meet."
"775","775/1009- (Sahih Mawquf) From Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah who said: My father said to me: ""O my son! If you are in a gathering from which you hope for good, and a need hastens you away, then say: Peace be upon you; for you will share with them in what they attained in that gathering. And there are no people who sit in a gathering and then disperse from it without mentioning Allah, except that it is as if they dispersed from a donkey's carcass."
"776","776/1010- (Authentic as mawquf, and authentic as marfu‘) from Abu Huraira; that he said: ""Whoever meets his brother, let him greet him with salam; then if a tree or a wall comes between them, and then he meets him again, let him greet him with salam."
"777","777/1011- (Sahih) From Anas ibn Malik: ""The companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him, would be walking and a tree would come before them, so a group of them would go to its right and a group to its left, and when they met again, they would greet each other with salam."
"778","778/1012- (Authentic chain of narration) From Thabit Al-Banani: ""That Anas, when he would wake up in the morning, would anoint his hand with fragrant oil, for shaking hands with his brothers."
"779","779/1013- (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Amr: A man said: O Messenger of Allah! Which Islam is best? He said: ""You feed food, and you greet with peace those you know and those you do not know."
"780","780/1014- (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: That the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade sitting in courtyards and elevated places. The Muslims said: We cannot do without it, we are unable to avoid it. He said: ""If so, then give it its due right."" They said: ""And what is its due right?"" He said: ""Lowering the gaze, guiding the wayfarer, saying 'Yarhamuk Allah' to the one who sneezes and praises Allah, and returning the greeting."
"781","781/1018- (Authentic chain of narration) From al-Hasan [هو البصري] who said: ""There is no sanctity between you and the evildoer."
"782","782/1020- (Hasan) From Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, passed by a group of people among whom was a man wearing perfume. He looked at them, greeted them with peace, and turned away from the man. The man said, ""Did you turn away from me!?"" He said: ""Between his eyes is a burning coal.""(1)."
"783","783/1021- From Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi: A man came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, with a ring of gold on his hand, so the Prophet, peace be upon him, turned away from him. When the man saw his dislike, he went and threw away the ring, and took a ring of iron and wore it, then came to the Prophet, peace be upon him. He said: ""This is worse; this is the adornment of the people of the Fire."" So he went back, threw it away, and wore a ring of silver, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, remained silent about it."
"784","784/1023- (Authentic chain of narration) From Ibn Shihab: That Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz asked Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah: Why did Abu Bakr write: From Abu Bakr; the Caliph of the Messenger of Allah. Then after him, Umar wrote: From Umar ibn al-Khattab; the Caliph of Abu Bakr. Who was the first to write: Commander of the Believers (Amir al-Mu'minin)? He said: My grandmother al-Shifa’ told me—and she was among the first emigrant women, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, whenever he entered the market, he would enter upon her—she said: ""Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the governor of the two Iraqs: Send me two strong and noble men; I want to ask them about Iraq and its people. So the governor of the two Iraqs sent him Labid ibn Rabi‘ah and ‘Adi ibn Hatim. They arrived in Medina, tied their mounts in the courtyard of the mosque, then entered the mosque and found ‘Amr ibn al-‘As. They said to him: O ‘Amr! Seek permission for us to enter upon the Commander of the Believers; Umar. So ‘Amr jumped up and entered upon Umar. He said: Peace be upon you, O Commander of the Believers! Umar said: What made you use this title, O son of al-‘As? You must explain what you said. He said: Yes, Labid ibn Rabi‘ah and ‘Adi ibn Hatim arrived and said to me: Seek permission for us to enter upon the Commander of the Believers. I said: By Allah, you have named him rightly, for he is the commander, and we are the believers. So from that day, the title was used in correspondence."
"785","785/1024- (Authentic chain of narration) from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah(2) who said: Muawiya came for Hajj, his first Hajj while he was Caliph, and Uthman ibn Hunaif al-Ansari entered upon him and said: Peace be upon you, O Amir, and the mercy of Allah. The people of Sham found this strange and said: Who is this hypocrite who shortens the greeting to the Commander of the Believers? Uthman then knelt on his knee and said: O Commander of the Believers! These people have found fault with me for something you know better than they do. By Allah, I greeted Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman with this greeting, and none of them found fault with it. Muawiya then said to those from the people of Sham who spoke: ""Take it easy; indeed, some of what he says is true, but the people of Sham have been affected by these tribulations. They say: Do not shorten the greeting to our Caliph; and I think, O people of Madinah, that you say to the collector of charity: O Amir."
"786","786/1025- (Authentic chain) From Jabir who said: ""I entered upon al-Hajjaj, and I did not greet him with salam."
"787","787/1026- (Authentic chain) From Tamim ibn Huzlam who said: I remember the first one who gave salam to someone with the title of leadership in Kufa. Al-Mughira ibn Shu’ba came out from the Rahba gate, and a man from Kindah— they claimed he was Abu Qurrah al-Kindi— met him and greeted him. He said: Peace be upon you, O commander, and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you. He disliked it. So he said: Peace be upon you, O commander, and the mercy of Allah, peace be upon you. Am I not one of them, or not? Simak said: ""Then he later acknowledged it."
"788","788/1028- (Sahih) From Al-Miqdad ibn Al-Aswad who said: ""The Prophet, peace be upon him, used to come at night and give greetings in a manner that would not wake a sleeping person, but would be heard by one who was awake."
"789","789/1030- (Sahih) From Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Fatima came walking, and her manner of walking was like the manner of walking of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. He said: ""Welcome, my daughter."" Then he seated her on his right, or on his left."
"790","790/1031- (Sahih) From Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Ammar sought permission to enter upon the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him – and he recognized his voice – so he said: ""Welcome to the good and purified one."
"791","791/1023- (Authentic chain of narration) From Abdullah ibn Amr who said: While we were sitting with the Prophet, peace be upon him, in the shade of a tree between Mecca and Medina—a Bedouin came, one of the roughest and harshest of people, and said: Peace be upon you. They replied: ""And upon you [السلام] ."
"792","792/1033- (Authentic chain) From Abu Jamrah(1): ""I heard Ibn Abbas, when greeted with salam, say: 'And upon you, and the mercy of Allah.'"
"793","793/1034- (Hasan Sahih) Abu Abdullah said: Qeila said: A man said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah! He said: ""And upon you be peace, and the mercy of Allah."
"794","794/1035- (Sahih) From Abu Dharr who said: I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, when he had finished his prayer, and I was the first to greet him with the greeting of Islam. He said: ""And upon you, and the mercy of Allah. From whom are you?"" I said: From Ghifar."
"795","795/1037- (Sahih) From Muawiya ibn Qurra who said: My father said to me: ""O my son! If a man passes by you and says: 'As-salamu alaykum,' do not say: 'Wa alayk,' as if you are specifying him alone; for he is not alone, but rather say: 'As-salamu alaykum.'"
"796","796/1038- (Authentic chain, mawquf) From Abdullah ibn al-Samit who said: I said to Abu Dharr: I passed by Abdur Rahman ibn Umm al-Hakam, and I greeted him with salam, but he did not reply anything to me. He said: ""O son of my brother! What harm is there upon you from that? One who is better than him has replied to you; an angel on his right."
"797","797/1039- (Authentic as mawquf – and authentic as marfu‘) from Abdullah [ هو ابن مسعود] who said: ""Indeed, as-Salam is one of the names of Allah, which Allah has placed on the earth, so spread it among yourselves. When a man greets a people with salam and they reply to him, they have a higher rank because he reminded them of as-Salam. And if they do not reply to him, someone better and purer than them will reply to him."
"798","798/1010- (Authentic chain of narration) From al-Hasan [هو البصري] who said: ""Initiating the greeting is voluntary, and responding to it is obligatory.""(1)."
"799","799/1042- (Authentic chain as mawquf, and authentic as marfu‘) From Abu Huraira who said: ""The stingiest of people is the one who is stingy with greeting (salam), and the most incapable of people is the one who is incapable of supplication (du‘a)."
"800","800/1043- (Sahih) From Anas ibn Malik: that he passed by some boys and greeted them with salam, and said: ""The Prophet, peace be upon him, used to do that with them."
"801","801/1044- (Authentic chain) From Anbasa [هو ابن عمار ] who said: ""I saw Ibn Umar greeting the children in the kuttab (school)."
"802","802/1045- (Sahih) From Umm Hani who said: I went to the Prophet, peace be upon him, while he was bathing, and I greeted him with salam [عليه] (1), so he said: ""Who is this?"" I said: Umm Hani. He said: ""Welcome [بأم هانئ] (2)."
"803","803/1046- (Hasan al-Isnad) From al-Hasan [هو البصري] who said: ""The women used to greet the men with salam."
"804","804/1047-(Authentic without mention of the hand) from Asma (1): That the Prophet, peace be upon him, passed by in the mosque, and a group of women were sitting, and he gestured to them with peace, and said: ""Beware of being ungrateful to the benefactors, beware of being ungrateful to the benefactors."" One of them said: We seek refuge with Allah—O Prophet of Allah—from being ungrateful for Allah’s blessings. He said: ""Yes, indeed, one of you may remain unmarried for a long time, then she becomes angry and says: By Allah, I have never seen any good from him at all. That is ingratitude for Allah’s blessings, and that is ingratitude for [نعم] the benefactors."
"805","805/1049 (Sahih) From Tariq(1) who said: We were sitting with Abdullah, when his doorkeeper came [فقال] (2) and said: The prayer has been established. So he stood up and we stood with him and entered the mosque. He saw the people bowing in the front of the mosque, so he said the takbir and bowed, and we walked and did as he did(3). A man passed by quickly(4) and said: Peace be upon you, O Abu Abdur Rahman! He said: Allah has spoken the truth, and His Messenger has conveyed (the message). When we finished the prayer, he returned, entered upon his family, and we remained in our place waiting for him to come out. Some of us said to others: Which of you will ask him? Tariq said: I will ask him. So he asked him, and he said: From the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Among the signs before the Hour: greeting only the specific (people), widespread trade until a woman helps her husband in trade, severing of family ties, widespread use of the pen(5), the appearance of false testimony, and concealing the testimony of truth."
"806","806/1051 (Sahih) from Anas: ""I was ten years old when the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, arrived in Medina, so my mothers would prepare me for his service, and I served him for ten years, and he died when I was twenty. Thus, I was the most knowledgeable of people regarding the matter of the veil. The first thing that was revealed was when the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, consummated his marriage with Zaynab bint Jahsh; he spent the morning with her as a bride, then invited the people, and they ate from the food, then they left, but a group remained with the Prophet, peace be upon him, and prolonged their stay. So he stood up and went out, and I went out with him so that they would leave. He walked and I walked with him until he came to the threshold of Aisha's room, then he thought they had left, so he returned and I returned [معه] until he entered upon Zaynab, and they were still sitting. So he went back and I went back until he reached the threshold of Aisha's room. He thought they had left, so he returned and I returned with him, and they had indeed left. The Prophet, peace be upon him, then placed a curtain between me and him, and the veil was revealed.""(3)"
"807","807/1052 (authentic chain) from Tha‘labah ibn Abi Malik al-Qurazi: He said: He rode to ‘Abd Allah ibn Suwayd – the brother of Banu Harithah ibn al-Harith – to ask him about the three times of privacy, and he used to act upon them. He said: ""What do you want?"" I said: I want to act upon them. He said: 'When I put down my clothes at midday, none of my family who has reached puberty enters upon me except with my permission, unless I call him, for that is his permission. Nor when dawn breaks and people begin to move, until the prayer is performed. Nor when I have prayed ‘Isha and put down my clothes until I sleep.'"
"808","808/1053 (Sahih) From Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I was eating hais with the Prophet, peace be upon him, and Umar passed by, so he called him and he ate. His hand touched my finger, so he said: 'Hiss!' If he had obeyed regarding you (women), no eye would have seen you. Then the verse of hijab was revealed."
"809","809/1054 (Sahih) From Umm Sabiyyah(3) bint Qais – and she is: Khawlah, the grandmother of Kharijah ibn al-Harith – she said: ""My hand and the hand of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, reached into the same vessel."
"810","810/1055 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: ""When one enters an uninhabited house, let him say: Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah."
"811","811/1056 (authentic chain) From Ibn Abbas who said: ""Do not enter houses other than your own until you seek permission and greet their inhabitants"" [النور: 27] , and he made an exception from that, saying: ""There is no blame upon you for entering houses not inhabited in which there is some belonging of yours... "" up to His saying: ""you conceal"" [النور: 29] ."
"812","812/1058 (Sahih al-Isnad) from Ibn Umar: ""When any of his children reached puberty, he would separate him; so he would not enter upon him except with permission."
"813","813/1059 (Authentic chain) From Alqama who said: A man came to Abdullah and said: ""Should I ask permission before entering upon my mother?"" He replied: ""Not at all times does she wish to see you."
"814","814/1060 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Muslim ibn Nudhail who said: A man asked Hudhayfah, saying: ""Should I seek permission to enter upon my mother?"" He replied: ""If you do not seek her permission, you will see what you dislike."" (And in another narration: what will upset you/1090)."
"815","815/1063 (Authentic chain) From Ata’ who said: I asked Ibn Abbas, and said: “Should I seek permission before entering upon my sister?” He said: “Yes.” I repeated, and said: “Two sisters are under my care, and I provide for them and spend on them; should I seek permission before entering upon them?” He said: “Yes. Do you like to see them naked?! Then he recited: ?O you who have believed, let those whom your right hands possess seek permission of you…? up to: ?three times of privacy for you? [النور: 58] He said: They were only commanded to seek permission during these three times of privacy.” He said: ?And when the children among you reach puberty…? [النور: 59] Ibn Abbas said: “Then seeking permission is obligatory, [على الناس كلهم] .”"
"816","816/1065 (Sahih) From Ubayd ibn Umair: That Abu Musa al-Ash'ari sought permission to enter upon Umar ibn al-Khattab, but he was not granted permission—and it was as if Umar was busy—so Abu Musa returned. When Umar was free, he said: Did I not hear the voice of Abdullah ibn Qais? Allow him in. It was said: He has already left. So he called for him and said: We were commanded to do that(1). Umar said: Bring me proof for that(2). So he went to the assembly of the Ansar and asked them. They said: None will testify to this for you except the youngest of us: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri. So he brought Abu Sa'id. Umar said: Has something from the matter of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, been hidden from me [هذا] ? The trading in the markets has distracted me. Meaning: going out for trade."
"817","817/1066 (Authentic chain) From Abu Huraira – regarding someone who seeks permission before giving salam – he said: ""He is not to be granted permission until [يأتي بالمفتاح/1067 و1083] he begins with salam."
"818","818/1068 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""If a man were to look into your house, and you threw a pebble at him and put out his eye, there would be no blame on you."
"819","819/1069 (Sahih) From Anas who said: ""The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was standing and praying when a man looked into his house, [ وفي طريق آخر: من خلل (وفي رواية : فألقم عينه خصاصة الباب/1091)(1) في حجرة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم /1072] so he took an arrow from his quiver and aimed it toward his eye [ليفقأ عينه] [فأخرج الرجل رأسه] . (In another narration: So the Bedouin shrank back and left, and he said: 'If you had remained, I would have gouged out your eye.')"
"820","820/1070 (Sahih) From Sahl ibn Sa'd: That a man looked through a hole in the door of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and with the Prophet, peace be upon him, was a comb with which he was scratching his head. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, saw him, he said: ""If I had known you were looking, I would have poked your eye with it."" And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Permission has only been prescribed because of the sight."
"821","821/1073 (Sahih li-ghayrihi) from Ubayd ibn Umayr(1), from Abu Musa who said: I sought permission to enter upon Umar, but I was not granted permission—three times—so I turned away. He sent for me and said: O Abdullah! Was it hard on you to wait at my door? Know that it is likewise hard for people to be kept waiting at your door. I said: Rather, I sought permission to enter upon you three times, and when I was not granted permission, I turned back [وكنا نؤمر بذلك] (2). He said: From whom did you hear this? I said: I heard it from the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace. He said: Did you hear from the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, what we did not hear? If you do not bring me proof for this, I will make you an example! So I went out until I came to a group of the Ansar sitting in the mosque, and I asked them. They said: Does anyone doubt this? I informed them of what Umar had said. They said: Only the youngest of us will go with you. So Abu Sa'id al-Khudri—or Abu Mas'ud—stood up with me and went to Umar. He said: We went out with the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, when he intended to visit Sa'd ibn Ubadah, until he came to him. He gave salam, but was not granted permission. Then he gave salam a second time, then a third, but was not granted permission. He said: ""We have fulfilled our duty,"" then turned back. Sa'd caught up with him and said: O Messenger of Allah! By the One Who sent you with the truth, I did not hear you give salam even once except that I heard it and responded to you, but I wished that you would increase your salam upon me and my household. Abu Musa said(3): By Allah, I was indeed trustworthy regarding the hadith of the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace. He said: Yes, but I wished to verify."
"822","822/1074 (Sahih Mawquf) from Abdullah [ هو ابن مسعود] who said: ""If a man is invited, then permission has been granted to him."
"823","823/1057 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""If one of you is invited, and he comes with the messenger, then that is his permission."
"824","824/1076 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""A man's messenger to another man is his permission."
"825","825/1077 (Sahih) From Abu al-‘Alaniyyah who said: I came to Abu Sa‘id al-Khudri, and greeted him with salam, but I was not given permission. Then I greeted him again, but I was not given permission. Then I greeted him a third time and raised my voice, saying: Peace be upon you, O people of the house! But I was not given permission, so I moved aside and sat down. Then a boy came out to me and said: Enter. So I entered, and Abu Sa‘id said to me: ""If you had increased (your greetings), you would not have been given permission."" Then I asked him about the vessels, and I did not ask him about anything except that he said: ""It is forbidden,"" until I asked him about the jaf. He said: ""It is forbidden."" Muhammad said: ""A lead is placed on its top, and it is tied."
"826","826/1078 (Hasan Sahih) from Abdullah ibn Busr, companion of the Prophet, peace be upon him: [ أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم] "" [كان] (1) When he came to a door wanting to seek permission, he would not face it directly; he would come from the right or the left; if permission was granted to him, otherwise he would leave."
"827","827/1079 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj who said: I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and I asked permission to enter upon him. They said to me: Stay where you are until he comes out to you. So I sat near his door. He came out to me and called for water, then performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks. I said: O Commander of the Faithful! Is this because of urine? He said: ""Because of urine, or anything else."
"828","828/1080 (Sahih) from Anas ibn Malik: ""The doors of the Prophet, peace be upon him, used to be knocked with fingernails."
"829","829/1081 (Sahih) From Kulayda ibn Hanbal: That Safwan ibn Umayya sent him to the Prophet, peace be upon him, during the conquest with milk, a young goat, and daghabis—Abu Asim said: meaning herbs—and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was at the upper part of the valley, and I had not embraced Islam nor sought permission. He said: ""Go back and say: Peace be upon you. May I enter?"" And that was after Safwan had embraced Islam."
"830","830/1084 (Sahih) From a man of Banu Amir who came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: ""Shall I enter?"" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to the servant girl: ""Go out and say to him: Say: Peace be upon you, may I enter? For he did not do well in seeking permission."" He said: I heard her before the servant girl came out to me, so I said: Peace be upon you, may I enter? He said: ""And upon you, enter."" He said: So I entered and said: For what purpose have you come? He said: ""I have not come to you except with good; I have come to you so that you worship Allah alone with no partner, and abandon the worship of Al-Lat and Al-Uzza, and that you pray five prayers in the night and day, and fast a month in the year, and perform pilgrimage to this House, and take from the wealth of your rich and return it to your poor."" He said: So I said to him: Is there any knowledge that you do not know? He said: ""Allah has taught much good, and among the knowledge is that which none knows except Allah; the five, none knows them except Allah: Indeed, Allah alone has knowledge of the Hour, and sends down the rain, and knows what is in the wombs, and no soul knows what it will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in what land it will die."" [لقمان: 34] ."
"831","831/1085 (Sahih al-Isnad) From Ibn Abbas who said: Umar sought permission to enter upon the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: ""Peace be upon the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon you, may Umar enter?"
"832","832/1086 (Sahih) From Jabir who said: I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, regarding a debt that was upon my father, so I knocked on the door. He said: ""Who is it?"" I said: ""It is I."" He said: ""'It is I, it is I?!'"" as if he disliked it."
"833","833/1088 (authentic chain) From Abdur Rahman ibn Jad'an who said: I was with Abdullah ibn Umar, and he sought permission to enter upon the people of a house. It was said: ""Enter in peace,"" but he refused to enter upon them(2)"
"834","834/1090 (authentic chain) from Muslim ibn Nadhir who said: A man sought permission to enter upon Hudhayfah, so he peeked in and said: ""Shall I enter?"" Hudhayfah said: ""As for your eye, it has entered, but as for your backside, it has not entered."
"835","835/1093 (authentic except for the sentence about leading prayer) from Thawban, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""It is not permissible for a Muslim man to look into the interior of a house until he seeks permission; if he does so, he has entered. And he should not lead a people in prayer and single himself out with a supplication to the exclusion of them until he departs. And he should not pray while he is restraining himself until he relieves himself."" Abu Abdullah said: The most authentic narration in this chapter is this hadith."
"836","836/1094 (Sahih) From Abu Umamah who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Three, all of them are guaranteed by Allah: if he lives, he is sufficed, and if he dies, he enters Paradise: Whoever enters his house in peace, he is guaranteed by Allah, Mighty and Majestic. And whoever goes out to the mosque, he is guaranteed by Allah. And whoever goes out in the way of Allah, he is guaranteed by Allah."
"837","837/1095 (authentic chain) From Abu al-Zubair; he heard Jabir say: ""When you enter upon your family, greet them with peace; a greeting from Allah, blessed and good."" He said: I never saw him except that he considered it obligatory(1) because of His saying: ?And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet in return with what is better than it or return it? [النساء: 86] ."
"838","838/1096 (Sahih) From Jabir; that he heard the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""When a man enters his house and mentions Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, at his entrance and at his food, Satan says: 'You have no lodging and no dinner.' But if he enters and does not mention Allah at his entrance, Satan says: 'You have caught lodging.' And if he does not mention Allah at his food, Satan says: 'You have caught lodging and dinner.'"
"839","839/1098 (Sahih al-Isnad) from Mujahid who said: Ibn Umar would not seek permission to enter the houses of the marketplace."
"840","840/1099 (Authentic chain) From Ata’ who said: ""Ibn Umar used to ask permission to enter the cloth merchant’s canopy."
"841","841/1101 (Sahih) From Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: Abu Musa wrote to Ruhban(1), greeting him with peace in his letter. It was said to him: ""Do you greet him with peace while he is a disbeliever?"" He said: ""He wrote to me and greeted me with peace, so I returned the greeting to him."
"842","842/1102 (Sahih) From Abu Basrah al-Ghifari, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Tomorrow I am going to ride to the Jews, so do not initiate the greeting of peace with them; but if they greet you, then say: And upon you.""(1)."
"843","843/1103 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""If you meet [the People of the Book (and in another narration: the polytheists), do not initiate the greeting of peace with them, and force them to the narrowest part of the road."
"844","844/1104 (Sahih) From Alqama who said: ""Abdullah [ هو ابن مسعود] only greeted the dihqan(1) with a gesture."
"845","845/1105 (Sahih) From Anas who said: A Jew passed by the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: ""As-sam 'alaykum"" (death be upon you), and his companions returned the greeting! He said: ""He said: 'As-sam 'alaykum,'"" so the Jew was taken and he admitted it. He said: ""Return to him what he said."
"846","846/1106 (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Umar; he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""When one of the Jews greets you, he only says: 'As-sam upon you.' So say: 'And upon you.'"
"847","847/1107 (Hasan) from Ibn Abbas who said: ""Return the greeting of peace to whoever it may be, whether he is a Jew, a Christian, or a Magian; that is because Allah says: ?And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet in return with what is better than it, or at least return it? [النساء: 86] ."
"848","848/1108 (Sahih) From Usama ibn Zayd: ""That the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, rode on a donkey that had a saddle covered with a Fadakiyah velvet cloth, and he seated Usama ibn Zayd behind him; he went to visit Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah, until he passed by a gathering in which was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul—and that Abdullah had become Muslim (2)—and in the gathering were a mixture of Muslims, polytheists, and idol worshippers, so he greeted them with peace."
"849","849/1109 (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Abbas: That Abu Sufyan ibn Harb informed him, Heraclius the king of Rome sent for him, then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, which [أرسل به] (1) was with Dihyah al-Kalbi to the chief of (Busra), so he handed it to Heraclius and he read it, and in it was: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Heraclius, the chief of the Romans. Peace be upon those who follow the guidance. To proceed; I invite you with the call of Islam. Submit and you will be safe; Allah will grant you your reward twice; but if you turn away, then upon you is the sin of the Arians. And: O People of the Book, come to a word that is just between us and you... to His saying: bear witness that we are Muslims [آل عمران: 64] ."
"850","850/1110 (Sahih) From Jabir who said: Some people from the Jews greeted the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: ""As-sam 'alaykum"" (death be upon you). He replied: ""And upon you."" Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, became angry and said: ""Did you not hear what they said?"" He said: ""Yes, I have [ سمعت فـ ] (2) replied to them. We respond to them, but they do not respond to us."
"851","851/1112 (Hasan) from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani: That he passed by a man whose appearance was that of a Muslim, so he greeted him with salam, and the man replied: ""And upon you be peace, and the mercy of Allah, and His blessings."" Then a boy said to him: ""He is a Christian!"" So Uqbah got up and followed him until he caught up with him. He said: ""Indeed, the mercy of Allah and His blessings are upon the believers, but may Allah prolong your life, and increase your wealth and your children.""(1)"
"852","852/1113 (Sahih) From Ibn Abbas who said: ""If Pharaoh were to say to me: 'May Allah bless you,' I would say: 'And you as well,' and Pharaoh has died."
"853","853/1115- From Abdur Rahman [هو ابن محمد بن زيد بن جدعان] who said: Ibn Umar passed by a Christian, so he greeted him with salam, and the Christian replied to him. Then he was informed that he was a Christian, so when he knew, he went back and said: ""Return my salam to me."
"854","854/1117 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Ibn Abbas who said: ""Indeed, I see that replying to a letter is a right just as replying to salam."
"855","855/1118 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Aisha bint Talha who said: I said to Aisha – while I was in her care – and people used to come to her from every region, so the elders would visit me(1) because of my position with her, and the youth would befriend me(2), so they would gift me and write to me from the regions. So I would say to Aisha: O aunt! This is the letter of so-and-so and his gift. So Aisha would say to me: ""O daughter! Then respond to him and reward him; and if you have nothing to reward with, I will give you."" She said: So she would give me."
"856","856/1119 (Authentic chain of narration) From Abdullah ibn Dinar; that Abdullah ibn Umar wrote to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan pledging allegiance to him, so he wrote to him: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, to Abd al-Malik, Commander of the Faithful, from Abdullah ibn Umar: Peace be upon you; indeed, I praise Allah to you, there is no deity except Him, and I acknowledge to you hearing and obedience according to the Sunnah of Allah and the Sunnah of His Messenger, as much as I am able."
"857","857/1120 (Authentic chain) From Zayd ibn Aslam who said: My father sent me to Ibn Umar, and I saw him writing: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. To proceed: ""."
"858","858/1121 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Hisham ibn Urwah who said: I saw letters from the letters of the Prophet, peace be upon him; whenever a section ended, he said: ""Amma ba'd."
"859","From the elders of the family of Zayd ibn Thabit, that Zayd ibn Thabit wrote with this letter: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, to Abdullah Muawiya, Commander of the Faithful, from Zayd ibn Thabit. Peace be upon you, Commander of the Faithful, and the mercy of Allah. Indeed, I praise to you Allah, there is no deity except Him. To proceed."
"860","From Abu Mas‘ud al-Jurayri who said: A man asked al-Hasan about the recitation of “Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.” He said: That is at the beginning of letters."
"861","861/1124 (Authentic chain) From Nafi‘ who said: Ibn ‘Umar had a need with Mu‘awiya, so he wanted to write to him. They said: Begin with him! They kept insisting until he wrote: ""(In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate) to Mu‘awiya."
"862","862/1125 (Authentic chain) From Anas ibn Sirin who said: I wrote to Ibn Umar, and he said: Write (In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful), as for what follows: to so-and-so."" And in another narration from him he said: ""A man wrote in the presence of Ibn Umar (In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful) to so-and-so, so Ibn Umar forbade him and said: 'Say: In the name of Allah, it is for him.'"" (1)."
"863","863/1129 (Sahih) From Mahmoud ibn Labid who said: When the median vein of Sa'd was injured on the day of the Trench and his condition worsened, they moved him to a woman called Rufaydah, and she used to treat the wounded. So whenever the Prophet, peace be upon him, passed by him, he would say: ""How are you this evening?"" and when morning came: ""How are you this morning?"" and he would inform him."
"864","864/1130 (Sahih) From Ibn Abbas: That Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came out from the presence of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, during his illness in which he passed away. The people said: O Abu al-Hasan! How did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, wake up this morning? He said: ""He woke up, by the praise of Allah, recovering."" He said: Then Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib took him by the hand and said: Do you see? By Allah, after three days you will be a subject of the staff, and by Allah, I see that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, will pass away in this illness of his; I know the faces of the sons of Abd al-Muttalib at death. So let us go to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and ask him about whom this matter (leadership) will be. If it is among us, we will know that, and if it is among others, we will speak to him, so that he may advise regarding us. Ali said: By Allah, if we ask him and he withholds it from us, the people will never give it to us after him, and by Allah, I will never ask the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, about it ever."
"865","865/1131 (Hasan al-isnad except for the addition, which is sahih al-isnad) from Abu al-Zinad that he took this letter from Kharija ibn Zayd and from the elders of the family of Zayd: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. To the servant of Allah; Muawiya, Commander of the Faithful, from Zayd ibn Thabit. Peace be upon you, Commander of the Faithful, and the mercy of Allah; I praise to you Allah, there is no god but He. To proceed: You ask me about the inheritance of the grandfather and the brothers… then he mentioned the letter(1). And we ask Allah for guidance, protection, and steadfastness in all our affairs, and we seek refuge in Allah from going astray, or being ignorant, or being burdened with what we have no knowledge of. And peace be upon you, Commander of the Faithful, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings and His forgiveness [وطيب صلواته/1001] ."
"866","866/1132 (Authentic as mawquf, and established as marfu‘) from Anas ibn Malik: He heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and a man greeted him with salam, so he returned the greeting. Then Umar asked the man: How are you? The man replied: I praise Allah to you. Umar said: ""This is what I wanted from you."
"867","867/1133 (Hasan li-ghayrihi) from Jabir ibn Abdullah: It was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him: How did you wake up? He said: ""In goodness; among a people who did not attend a funeral, nor visit a sick person."
"868","868/1134 (Hasan al-isnad, mawquf) from Muhajir – he is: al-Sa’igh – who said: I used to sit with a man from among the companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him, a large man from the Hadramis, and when it was said to him: How did you wake up? He would say: ""We do not associate anything with Allah."
"869","869/1135 (Authentic by other than itself as mawquf, and it has been authenticated as marfu‘) from Hudhayfah who said: ""O ‘Umar ibn Sali‘! If you see Qays gathering together in al-Sham, then beware, beware. By Allah, Qays will not leave a believing servant of Allah except that they frighten him or kill him. And by Allah, a time will surely come upon them when they will not be able to prevent even the tail of a sheep.""(1)."
"870","1136 / 870 (Sahih) From Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was informed of a funeral. It was as if he delayed until the people had taken their seats, then he came with him. When the people saw him, they hurried to make room for him, and some of them stood up from their places so he could sit in their seat. He said: No, indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""The best of gatherings are the most spacious."" Then he moved aside and sat in a spacious place."
"871","871/1138 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him: ""If one of you gets up from his seat, then returns to it; he has more right to it."
"872","872/1139 (Sahih) From Anas: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to us while we were boys, and he greeted us with peace, and he sent me on an errand, and he sat in the road waiting for me until I returned to him. He said: I was late for Umm Sulaym. She said: What kept you? I said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, sent me on an errand. She said: What is it? I said: It is a secret. She said: ""Then keep the secret of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him."
"873","Ibn Umar reported: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""None of you should make another man get up from his seat and then sit in it himself, but rather make room and expand for one another."
"874","874/1141 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Jabir ibn Samurah who said: ""When we came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, each of us would sit wherever he found a place."
"875","875/1142 (Hasan) from Abdullah ibn Amr; that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""It is not permissible for a man to separate between two people except with their permission."
"876","876/1144 (Sahih) From al-Sha'bi who said: A man came to Abdullah ibn Amr—while the people were sitting with him—stepping over to reach him, but they prevented him. He said: Leave the man. So he came and sat with him and said: Tell me something you heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: ""The Muslim is the one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the emigrant is the one who abandons what Allah has forbidden."
"877","877/1145 (Sahih al-Isnad) from Ibn Abbas: ""The most honored of people to me is my companion who sits with me."
"878","878/1147 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Kathir ibn Murrah who said: I entered the mosque on Friday and found Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i sitting in a circle, stretching out his legs in front of him. When he saw me, he drew in his legs, then said to me: ""Do you know why I stretched out my legs? So that a righteous man would come and sit."
"879","879/1148 (Hasan) From al-Harith ibn Amr al-Sahmi who said: I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, while he was at Mina – or at Arafat – and the people had gathered around him, and the Bedouins would come, and when they saw his face, they would say: ""This is a blessed face."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah! Ask forgiveness for me."" He said: ""O Allah, forgive us."" So I went around and said: ""Ask forgiveness for me."" He said: ""O Allah, forgive us."" So I went around and said: ""Ask forgiveness for me."" He said: ""O Allah, forgive us."" Then he took [يبزق، فقال] )(1) with his hand [فأخذ بها] (1) his saliva, and wiped it on his sandal, disliking that it should touch anyone around him."
"880","880/1149 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: That the Prophet, peace be upon him, forbade sitting in the pathways. They said: O Messenger of Allah! It is difficult for us to sit in our houses. He said: ""If you must sit, then give the pathways their due."" They said: And what is their due, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Lowering the gaze, responding to greetings, guiding the one who is lost, enjoining what is right, and forbidding what is wrong."
"881","881/1150 (Sahih) From Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Beware of sitting in the roads."" They said: O Messenger of Allah! We have no choice but to have our gatherings in them to talk. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If you must, then give the road its right."" They said: And what is the right of the road, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Lowering the gaze, refraining from harm, enjoining what is right, and forbidding what is wrong."
"882","882/1151 (Sahih) From Abu Musa al-Ash'ari who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, went out one day to a garden from the gardens of Medina for his need, and I went out following him. When he entered the garden, I sat at its door and said: ""Today I will be the doorkeeper of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him,"" though he did not command me (1). The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, went and fulfilled his need, then sat on the edge of the well, uncovered his shins, and dangled them in the well. Then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came seeking permission to enter. I said: ""Wait until I seek permission for you."" So he stood, and I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah! Abu Bakr seeks permission to enter."" He said: ""Give him permission, and give him glad tidings of Paradise."" So he entered and sat on the right of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, uncovered his shins, and dangled them in the well. Then Umar came, and I said: ""Wait until I seek permission for you."" The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Give him permission, and give him glad tidings of Paradise."" Umar came and sat on the left of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, uncovered his shins, and dangled them in the well, so the edge was filled, and there was no more space. Then Uthman came, and I said: ""Wait until I seek permission for you."" The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Give him permission, and give him glad tidings of Paradise along with a trial that will befall him."" He entered, but did not find a place with them, so he moved until he came opposite them, at the edge of the well, uncovered his shins, then dangled them in the well. I began to wish that a brother of mine would come, and I prayed to Allah that he would come, but he did not come until they stood up. Ibn al-Musayyib said: I interpreted that as their graves; they were gathered here, and Uthman was alone."
"883","883/1152 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: The Prophet, peace be upon him, went out with a group [النهار] he did not speak to me and I did not speak to him, until he came to the market of Banu Qaynuqa‘, and he sat in the courtyard of Fatima’s house; he said: ""Is Luk‘a there? Is Luk‘a there?"" (3) She kept him back for a while, and I thought she was putting a necklace on him or washing him, then he came running until he embraced him and kissed him, and said: ""O Allah, love him, and love those who love him."
"884","884/1153 (Sahih) From Ibn Umar who said: ""The Prophet, peace be upon him, forbade that a man should make another stand up from his seat and then sit in it himself."" And whenever Ibn Umar was offered a seat by someone who stood up for him, he would not sit in it."
"885","885/1154 (authentic chain) from Anas: ""I served the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, one day, until when I saw that I had finished serving him, I said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, is taking a nap. So I left from him, and there were some boys playing, so I stood watching them and their play. The Prophet, peace be upon him, came and reached them, greeted them with salam, then called me and sent me on an errand. He stayed in the shade until I came to him. I was late returning to my mother, so she said: What kept you? I said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent me on an errand. She said: What is it? I said: It is a secret of the Prophet, peace be upon him. She said: Keep the secret of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. So I never told anyone about that errand, and if I were to tell anyone, I would have told you."
"886","886/1155 (Hasan li-ghayrihi) from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib; that he heard Abu Huraira describing the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: ""He was of medium stature(1), tending towards tallness, intensely white-skinned, with black beard hair, beautiful teeth(2), with long eyelashes(3), broad between the shoulders, with a wide forehead(4), he would place his entire foot on the ground, having no arch, he would approach entirely and depart entirely, I have not seen anyone like him before or after."
"887","887/1159 (Sahih) From Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Whoever creates an image(1) will be tasked to breathe life into it and will be punished, but he will not be able to breathe life into it. And whoever claims to have seen a dream(4) will be tasked to tie two grains of barley together and will be punished, but he will not be able to tie them together. And whoever listens to the conversation of a people [وهم] who try to avoid him, molten lead will be poured into his ears.""(5)"
"888","888/1161 (Authentic chain) From Abu Al-‘Aliyah who said: ""I sat with Ibn Abbas on a bed."" And in a narration from Abu Jamrah, he said: I used to sit with Ibn Abbas, and he would seat me on his bed, and he said to me: ""Stay with me until I assign for you a share from my wealth."" So I stayed with him for two months."
"889","889/1162 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Khalid ibn Dinar Abu Khulda who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik—while he was with al-Hakam, the governor in Basra, on the couch—say: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, when it was hot, would delay the prayer, and when it was cold, he would hasten the prayer."
"890","890/1163 (Hasan Sahih) From Anas ibn Malik who said: I entered upon the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, while he was on a bed woven with strips, with a pillow of leather stuffed with palm fibers under his head. Between his skin and the bed was a cloth. Umar entered upon him and wept. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: ""What makes you weep, O Umar?"" He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I do not weep except knowing that you are more honored by Allah than Kisra and Caesar, yet they indulge in what they indulge of the world, and you, O Messenger of Allah, are in the state that I see! The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Are you not pleased, O Umar, that for them is the world, and for us is the Hereafter?"" I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""It is so."
"891","891/1164 (Sahih) From Abu Rifa’ah al-‘Adawi who said: I reached the Prophet, peace be upon him, while he was delivering a sermon, so I said: O Messenger of Allah! A stranger has come to ask about his religion, he does not know what his religion is. So he turned to me and left his sermon. A chair was brought to him, I thought its legs were made of iron — Humayd said: I think it was black wood, he thought it was iron — so he sat on it, and began teaching me from what Allah had taught him, then he completed his sermon; that was the last of it(1)."
"892","892/1165m (Hasan al-Isnad) from Imran ibn Muslim who said: ""I saw Anas sitting on a bed, placing one of his legs over the other."
"893","893/1166 (Authentic chain of narration) from Sa'id al-Maqburi who said: I passed by Ibn Umar while he was with a man talking, so I approached them, and he struck my chest and said: ""If you find two people talking, do not stand with them, nor sit with them, until you seek their permission."" So I said: May Allah rectify you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman! I only hoped to hear some good from you both."
"894","894/1167 (Authentic chain, stopped; and it has been authenticated as raised) from Ibn Abbas who said: ""Whoever listens to the conversation of a people while they dislike it, molten lead will be poured into his ear; and whoever fabricates a dream will be tasked to tie a barley grain."
"895","895/1168 (Sahih) from Abdullah [ هو ابن عمر] ; that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If there are three people, then two of them should not converse privately excluding the third."
"896","896/1169 (Sahih) from Abdullah [ هو ابن مسعود] who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""If you are three, then two should not converse privately excluding the third [حتى يختلطوا بالناس/1171] ; for that would grieve him."
"897","897/1170 (Sahih) From Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, similar to it. We said(1): What if they are four? He said: ""It does not harm him, (and in another narration: there is no problem/1127)."
"898","898/1174 (Sahih) from Qais, from his father [ هو أبو حازم البجلي] : ""He came while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was delivering a sermon, so he stood in the sun, and he ordered him, so he moved to the shade."
"899","899/1175 (Sahih) From Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade two types of clothing and two types of sales: He forbade mulamasa and munabadhah in sales—mulamasa is when a man touches the garment, and munabadhah is when the other throws his garment to him—and that would be their sale without inspection. And the two types of clothing: ishtimal as-samma’—as-samma’ is to place the end of his garment on one of his shoulders, so that one side of his body is exposed with nothing on it—and the other type of clothing is to wrap himself with his garment while sitting, with nothing covering his private parts from it."
"900","900/1176 (Sahih) From Abu Qilabah who said: Abu al-Malih informed me, he said: I entered with your father, Zayd, upon Abdullah ibn Amr, and he narrated to us: That the Prophet, peace be upon him, was told about my fasting, so he came to me, and a cushion made of leather stuffed with fibers was thrown for him, so he sat on the ground, and the cushion was between me and him, and he said to me: ""Is it not sufficient for you to fast three days of every month?"" [قال: قلت: يا رسول الله!] He said: ""Five."" I said: O Messenger of Allah! [قلت: يا رسول الله! قال: "" سبعاً "" قلت: يا رسول الله! قال: "" تسعاً ""، قلت: يا رسول الله!] He said: ""Eleven."" I said: O Messenger of Allah! He said: ""There is no fasting above the fasting of Dawud; half of the time, fasting one day and breaking the fast one day."
"901","901/1177 (Authentic chain) from Abdullah ibn Busr: ""The Prophet, peace be upon him, passed by his father, so he threw a cloak for him and he sat on it."
"902","902/1178 (Hasan) from Qayla who said: ""I saw the Prophet, peace be upon him, sitting in the crouching position (qurfusā’), and when I saw the Prophet, peace be upon him, humble in his sitting, I trembled out of fear."
"903","903/1179 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Hanzala ibn Huzaym who said: ""I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and I saw him sitting cross-legged."
"904","904/1181 (Sahih al-Isnad) from Imran ibn Muslim who said: ""I saw Anas ibn Malik sitting like this—cross-legged—and placing one of his feet over the other."
"905","905/1182 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Sulaym ibn Jabir al-Hujaymi who said: I came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, while he was sitting with his knees drawn up in a cloak, and its fringes were on his feet. I said: O Messenger of Allah! Advise me. He said: ""Adhere to fearing Allah, and do not belittle any act of goodness, even if you pour from your bucket into the vessel of one seeking water, or you speak to your brother with a cheerful face. And beware of letting your lower garment hang down, for that is from arrogance, and Allah does not love it. And if a man reproaches you for something he knows about you, do not reproach him for something you know about him; let it be a burden upon him and a reward for you. And do not insult anything."" He said: So after that, I never insulted an animal nor a human being."
"906","906/1183 (Hasan) From Abu Huraira who said: I never saw Hasan except that my eyes overflowed with tears; and that is because the Prophet, peace be upon him, went out one day and found me in the mosque, so he took me by the hand and I went with him. He did not speak to me until we came to the market of Banu Qaynuqa', and he walked around in it and looked, then he turned back and I was with him until we came to the mosque. He sat and wrapped his arms around his knees, then said: ""Where is Luka‘? Call for me Luka‘."" So Hasan came running and fell into his lap, then he put his hand in his beard, then the Prophet, peace be upon him, began to open his mouth and put his mouth in his (Hasan's) mouth, then said: ""O Allah, I love him, so love him, and love those who love him."
"907","907/1184 (Hasan Sahih) From Anas ibn Malik: That the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, led them in the Zuhr prayer, and when he finished, he stood upon the pulpit and mentioned the Hour, and mentioned that there are great matters in it. Then he said: ""Whoever wishes to ask about anything, let him ask about it, for by Allah, you will not ask me about anything except that I will inform you of it, as long as I am in this position of mine."" Anas said: The people wept much when they heard that from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, kept saying: ""Ask."" So Umar knelt on his knees and said: We are pleased with Allah as our Lord, with Islam as our religion, and with Muhammad as our Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was silent when Umar said that. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""It is more appropriate(1). By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, Paradise and Hell were displayed before me across this wall – while I was praying – and I have never seen the like of today in good and evil."
"908","908/1185 (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Asim al-Mazni who said: ""I saw him – meaning: the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him – lying on his back, placing one of his legs over the other."
"909","909/1187 (Sahih) From Tukhfa al-Ghifari, who was one of the people of al-Suffa, he said: While I was sleeping in the mosque at the end of the night, someone came to me while I was sleeping on my stomach, and he moved me with his foot and said: ""Get up; this is a lying position that Allah hates."" So I raised my head, and behold, the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was standing at my head."
"910","910/1189 (Sahih) From Salim, from his father [عبد الله بن عمر ] who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""None of you should eat with his left hand, nor drink with his left hand; for Satan eats with his left hand and drinks with his left hand."" He said: Nafi‘ used to add to it: ""And do not take with it, nor give with it."
"911","911/1191 (Hasan al-Isnad, and it has been authentically reported as marfu‘) from Abu Umamah who said: ""Indeed, the Shaytan comes to the bed of one of you after his family has prepared and arranged it, and he throws upon it a stick, or a stone, or something; in order to make him angry with his family. So if he finds that, he should not become angry with his family."" He said: ""For that is from the work of Shaytan."
"912","912/1192 (Sahih) From Ali, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Whoever spends the night on the roof of a house without a covering(1), the protection is absolved from him."
"913","913/1194 (Hasan) From a man among the companions of the Prophet, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Whoever spends the night on a roof without a barrier and falls from it and dies, the responsibility is absolved from him; and whoever rides the sea when it is turbulent—meaning: raging—and perishes, the responsibility is absolved from him."
"914","914/1195 (Hasan Sahih) from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari: ""That the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was in a garden on the edge of a well, letting his legs hang down into the well."
"915","915/1199 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, would enter the morning, he would say: ""O Allah, by You we enter the morning, and by You we enter the evening, and by You we live, and by You we die, and to You is the resurrection."" And when he would enter the evening, he would say: ""O Allah, by You we enter the evening, and by You we enter the morning, and by You we live, and by You we die, and to You is the return."
"916","916/1200 (Sahih) From Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would not leave these words when he entered the morning and when he entered the evening: ""O Allah, I ask You for well-being in this world and the Hereafter. O Allah, I ask You for pardon and well-being in my religion and my worldly affairs, my family and my wealth. O Allah, conceal my faults and keep me safe from my fears. O Allah, protect me from in front of me and from behind me, from my right and from my left, and from above me, and I seek refuge in Your greatness from being taken unaware from beneath me."
"917","917/1202 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah! Teach me something to say when I wake up in the morning and when I go to bed in the evening. He said: ""Say: O Allah, Knower of the unseen and the seen, Creator of the heavens and the earth, Lord of everything and its Sovereign (1), I bear witness that there is no deity but You. I seek refuge in You from the evil of myself and from the evil of Satan and his partnership; say it when you wake up in the morning, when you go to bed in the evening, and when you take to your bed."
"918","918/1204 (Sahih) From Abu Rashid al-Hubarani: I came to Abdullah ibn Amr, and I said to him: Tell us what you heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. So he handed me a scroll and said: This is what the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, wrote for me. I looked in it and found in it: Indeed, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah! Teach me what I should say when I wake up in the morning and when I enter the evening. He said: ""O Abu Bakr! Say: O Allah, Creator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the seen, Lord of everything and its Sovereign, I seek refuge in You from the evil of myself, and from the evil of Satan and his partnership, and that I should commit evil against myself or bring it upon a Muslim."
"919","919/1205 (Sahih) From Hudhayfah who said: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, wanted to sleep, he would say: ""In Your name, O Allah, I die and I live."" And when he woke up from his sleep, he would say: ""Praise be to Allah who gave us life after He caused us to die, and to Him is the resurrection."
"920","920/1206 (Sahih) From Anas who said: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, would go to his bed, he would say: ""Praise be to Allah who has fed us, given us drink, sufficed us, and sheltered us. How many are there who have no one to suffice them or shelter them!"
"921","921/1207 (Authentic from the statement of Abu al-Zubair, so it is mawquf and disconnected) from Jabir who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would not sleep until he recited ?Alif Lam Meem, Tanzeel? [السجدة/1209] , and ?Tabarak alladhi biyadihi al-mulk?."" Abu al-Zubair said: ""So they surpass every surah in the Qur'an by seventy good deeds, and whoever recites them will have seventy good deeds written for him, seventy degrees raised for him, and seventy sins removed from him."
"922","922/1208 (Sahih Mawquf) Abdullah [ هو ابن مسعود] said: ""Sleep during remembrance is from Shaytan. If you wish, then try it: when one of you takes his bed and wants to sleep, let him remember Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."
"923","923/1212 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to say when he went to his bed: ""O Allah, Lord of the heavens and the earth, and Lord of all things, Splitter of the grain and the date-stone, Revealer of the Torah, the Gospel, and the Qur'an, I seek refuge in You from the evil of every possessor of evil whom You hold by the forelock. You are the First, so there is nothing before You, and You are the Last, so there is nothing after You, and You are the Most High, so there is nothing above You, and You are the Most Near, so there is nothing nearer than You. Settle my debt for me and enrich me from poverty."
"924","924/1213 (Sahih) From Al-Bara’ ibn ‘Azib who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would go to his bed, he would lie on his right side, then say: ""O Allah, I have submitted myself to You, and I have turned my face to You, and I have entrusted my affairs to You, and I have relied my back upon You; in desire and fear of You. There is no refuge and no escape from You except to You. I believe in Your Book which You have revealed, and in Your Prophet whom You have sent."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever says these words and then dies during that night, dies upon the natural disposition (fitrah)."
"925","925/1215 (Sahih) From Al-Bara’ who said: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, wanted to sleep, he would place his hand under his right cheek and say: ""O Allah, protect me from Your punishment on the day You resurrect Your servants.""(1)."
"926","926/1216 (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Two qualities which no Muslim man preserves except that he will enter Paradise, and they are easy, but those who act upon them are few."" It was said, ""And what are they, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""That one of you glorifies Allah after every prayer ten times, praises Him ten times, and magnifies Him ten times; that is one hundred and fifty on the tongue, and one thousand five hundred on the scale."" I saw the Prophet, peace be upon him, counting them with his hand. ""And when he goes to his bed, he glorifies Him, praises Him, and magnifies Him; that is one hundred on the tongue, and one thousand on the scale. So which of you commits in a day and night two thousand five hundred sins?"" It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah! How can one not preserve them?"" He said: ""Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and reminds him of such-and-such a need, so he does not remember them."
"927","927/1217 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""When one of you goes to his bed, let him take the inside of his garment and dust off his bed with it, and mention the name of Allah, for he does not know what may have come onto his bed after him. When he wants to lie down, let him lie on his right side and say: 'Glory be to You, my Lord, with You (in another narration: in Your name / 1210) I lay down my side and with You I raise it. If You take my soul, forgive it, and if You send it back, protect it as You protect Your righteous servants.'"
"928","928/1218 (Sahih) From Rabi‘ah ibn Ka‘b who said: I used to spend the night at the door of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and I would bring him his water for ablution. He said: I would hear him in the depths of the night saying: ""Sami‘a Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears the one who praises Him)."" And I would hear him in the depths of the night saying: ""Alhamdu lillahi Rabbil ‘alamin (All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds)."
"929","929/1219 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet ﷺ who said: ""Whoever sleeps while there is grease on his hand before washing it, and something befalls him, let him blame no one but himself."
"930","930/1220 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Whoever spends the night with grease on his hand, and something befalls him, let him blame no one but himself."
"931","931/1221 (Sahih) From Jabir ibn Abdullah; that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Shut the doors, tie the water skins, cover the vessels, and cover the containers(1), and extinguish the lamps; for indeed, Satan does not open a closed door, nor untie a knot, nor uncover a vessel, and indeed the vermin may set fire to people's houses."
"932","932/1222 (Sahih) From Ibn Abbas who said: A mouse came and started dragging the wick, so the servant girl went to drive it away, but the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Leave it."" Then it brought it and threw it onto the mat on which he was sitting, and it burned a spot the size of a dirham. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""When you go to sleep, extinguish your lamps, for Satan guides the likes of this (mouse) to the likes of this (wick) and burns you."
"933","933/1224 (Sahih) From Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Do not leave the fire in your houses when you sleep; [فإنها عدوّ / 1226] ."
"934","934/1225 (Authentic chain, Mawquf) From Ibn Umar who said: Umar said: ""Indeed, the fire is an enemy, so beware of it."" So Ibn Umar used to go around the fires of his family and extinguish them before going to bed."
"935","935/1227 (Sahih) From Abu Musa who said: A house in Medina burned down on its people during the night, and this was mentioned to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he said: ""Indeed, [هذه] (2) fire is an enemy to you; so when you go to sleep, extinguish it for your safety."
"936","936/1228 (Authentic chain, Mawquf) from Ibn Abbas: When the sky rained, he would say: ""O servant girl! Bring out my saddle, bring out my clothes,"" and he would say: ""And We sent down from the sky blessed water"" [ق: 9] ."
"937","937/1229 (Sahih) From Ibn Abbas: ""That the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered that the whip be hung in the house."
"938","938/1230 (Hasan) from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Beware of conversing late at night after the calm of the night; for none of you knows what Allah may spread of His creation. Close the doors, tie the water skins, cover the vessels, and extinguish the lamps."
"939","939/1231 (Sahih) From Jabir, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Keep your children in until the darkness—or the surge—of the evening passes; it is the hour when the devils spread."
"940","940/1232- From Ibn Umar: ""He disliked inciting animals against each other."
"941","941/1233 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Go out less after things have quieted down; for Allah has creatures He lets loose, so whoever hears the barking of a dog or the braying of a donkey [من الليل/1234] , let him seek refuge with Allah from the accursed devil; for they see what you do not see."" [وأجيفوا الأبواب، واذكروا اسم الله عليها ؛ فإن الشيطان لا يفتح باباً أجيف وذكر اسم الله عليه، وغطوا الجرار، وأوكئوا القرب، وأكفئوا الآنية/ 1234] ."
"942","942/1236 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that he said: ""If you hear the crowing of roosters at night, then it has seen an angel, so ask Allah from His bounty. And if you hear the braying of donkeys at night, then it has seen a devil, so seek refuge in Allah from the devil."
"943","943/1238 (Hasan al-Isnad) from al-Sa'ib [ هو ابن يزيد] , from Umar who said: ""Sometimes men, Quraysh, would sit at the door of Ibn Mas'ud, and when the shade shifted, he would say: 'Stand up(1), for what remains is for the devil,'"" and then he would not pass by anyone except that he would make him stand. He said: Then while he was like that, it was said: This is the freedman of Banu al-Hasshas reciting poetry, so he called him and said: How did you say it? He said: ""Bid Salayma farewell if you set out as a warrior... Gray hair and Islam are enough to deter a man."" He said: ""Enough for you, you have spoken the truth, you have spoken the truth."
"944","944/1239 (Hasan al-Isnad) And in a narration from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to pass by us at midday—or close to it—and say: ""Get up and take a nap, for what remains is for the devil."
"945","945/1240 (Sahih) From Anas who said: ""They would pray together, then take a midday nap."
"946","946/1241 (authentic chain) from Anas: The people of Medina had no drink—when wine was forbidden—more pleasing to them than dates and unripe dates; for I used to serve the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, while they were with Abu Talha. A man passed by and said: ""Wine has been forbidden."" They did not say: When? Or: Let us consider. They said: O Anas! Pour it out. Then they said at Umm Sulaym's: Cool yourselves and bathe. Then Umm Sulaym perfumed them, then they went to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and found the news as the man had said. Anas said: They never tasted it after that."
"947","947/1242 (authentic chain) from Khawat ibn Jubayr who said: ""Sleeping at the beginning of the day is foolishness(3), in the middle is nature(4), and at the end is stupidity."
"948","948/1234 (Sahih al-Isnad) From Maymun ibn Mihran who said: I asked Nafi‘: Did Ibn ‘Umar used to invite people for a banquet? He said: But once a camel of his was injured, so we slaughtered it, then he said: ""Gather for me the people of Madinah(1)!"" Nafi‘ said: So I said: O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman! For what reason? We have no bread. He said: ""O Allah, to You is all praise, this is ‘iraq(2), and this is broth, or he said: broth and some pieces(3), so whoever wishes may eat, and whoever wishes may leave."
"949","949/1244 From Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Ibrahim, peace be upon him, was circumcised at the age of eighty years, and he circumcised himself with an adze."" Abu Abdullah said: He means a place."
"950","950/1247 (Hasan) from Umm Alqama: ""The daughters of the brother of Aisha were circumcised, so it was said to Aisha: Shall we not call for them someone to entertain them? She said: Yes, so she sent for 'Adi(1) and he came to them. Aisha passed by in the house and saw him singing and moving his head in delight—and he had much hair—so she said: Uff, a devil! Take him out, take him out."
"951","951/1250 (Authentic chain, mawquf and maqtu‘, and authentically attributed to him as marfu‘) from Abu Huraira who said: ""Ibrahim, peace be upon him, was circumcised when he was one hundred and twenty years old, then lived after that for eighty years. Sa‘id [بن المسيب] said: 'Ibrahim was the first to be circumcised, the first to host guests, the first to trim the mustache, the first to cut the nails, and the first to have gray hair. He said: O Lord! What is this? He said: Dignity. He said: O Lord, increase me in dignity.'"
"952","952/1251 (Authentic chain, mawquf and mursal) from Al-Hasan [هو البصري] who said: ""Are you not amazed at this? – meaning: Malik ibn Al-Mundhir – he went to some elders from the people of (Kaskar) who had accepted Islam, so he examined them and ordered that they be circumcised, and it was winter, and I was informed that some of them died. Indeed, the Roman and the Abyssinian accepted Islam with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and nothing was examined about them."" (1)."
"953","953/1252 (Authentic chain, mawquf or maqtu‘) from Ibn Shihab who said: ""When a man accepted Islam, he was ordered to be circumcised even if he was an adult."
"954","954/1254 (Sahih) From Anas who said: I took Abdullah ibn Abi Talha to the Prophet, peace be upon him, on the day he was born, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was in a cloak, marking his camel. He said: ""Do you have some dates with you?"" I said: Yes. So I handed him some dates, and he chewed them, then he opened the mouth of the boy and put them in his mouth, and the boy began to smack his lips. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""The Ansar love dates,"" and he named him: Abdullah."
"955","955/1255 (Authentic chain, but disconnected) From Mu'awiya ibn Qurra who said: ""When Iyas was born to me, I invited a group of the companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and I fed them, and they supplicated. So I said: You have supplicated, so may Allah bless you in what you have supplicated for, and I will supplicate with a supplication, so say 'Ameen.' He said: So I supplicated for him with many supplications regarding his religion, his intellect, and so on. He said: Indeed, I recognize in him the effect of the supplication of that day."
"956","956/1256 (Hasan al-isnad, mawquf) from Kathir ibn Ubayd who said: ""When a child was born among them—meaning among her family—Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, would not ask: 'Is it a boy or a girl?' She would say: 'Was it created sound?' If it was said: 'Yes,' she would say: 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.'"
"957","957/1258 (Authentic chain, Mawquf) from Nafi‘: ""Ibn ‘Umar used to trim his nails every fifteen nights, and shave his pubic hair every month."
"958","958/1260 (Sahih al-Isnad Mawqufan) from Ibn Umar who said: ""Al-Maysir: gambling."
"959","959/1262 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever among you swears and says in his oath: By al-Lat and al-Uzza, let him say: There is no god but Allah. And whoever says to his companion: Come, let me gamble with you, let him give charity."
"960","960/1265 (Authentic chain, mawquf) from Ibn Abbas: Regarding His saying, the Mighty and Majestic: ? And of mankind is he who purchases idle talk? [لقمان: 6] he said: ""Singing and its likes."
"961","961/1266 (Hasan) from Al-Bara' ibn 'Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Spread peace and you will be safe, and frivolity is evil."" Abu Mu'awiyah said: Frivolity means mischief."
"962","962/1269 (Hasan) From Abu Musa al-Ash'ari; that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever plays with dice has disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."
"963","963/1270 (Sahih) From Abdullah ibn Mas'ud who said: ""Beware of these two marked dice; which are thrown forcefully; for indeed they are of maysir (gambling)."
"964","964/1271 (Hasan) from Buraidah [ابن الحصيب] , from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Whoever plays with nardashir, it is as if he has dyed his hands in the flesh and blood of a pig."
"965","965/1273 (Authentic chain, Mawquf) From Nafi‘: ""Abdullah ibn Umar, whenever he found any of his family playing with dice, he would strike him and break it."
"966","966/1274 (Hasan al-Isnad, Mawquf) from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her: It reached her that a family in her house—they were tenants in it—had dice, so she sent to them: ""If you do not remove it, I will evict you from my house,"" and she denounced that upon them."
"967","967/1275 (Hasan al-Isnad, Mawquf) from Kulthum ibn Jabr who said: Ibn al-Zubayr addressed us and said: ""O people of Mecca! It has reached me that some men from Quraysh are playing a game called nardashir—and he was left-handed(3)—he said: By Allah, ?Intoxicants and gambling? [المائدة: 90] , and I swear by Allah: if I am brought a man who played it, I will punish him in his hair and his skin, and I will give his belongings to whoever brings him to me."
"968","968/1277 (authentic chain, mawquf) from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As who said: ""The one who plays with the two stones as gambling is like the eater of pork, and the one who plays with them not as gambling is like the one who dips his hand in the blood of a pig."
"969","969/1278 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: That the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""A believer is not stung from the same hole twice."
"970","From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: Whoever attacks us at night is not one of us. [صحيح الأدب المفرد:970] Abu Abdullah said: There is some doubt regarding its chain of transmission."
"971","On the authority of Abu Huraira who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: Whoever carries weapons against us is not one of us."
"972","On the authority of Abu Musa who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: Whoever carries weapons against us is not one of us."
"973","973/1282 (Sahih) From Abu al-Malih, from a man of his people(1) – and he had companionship (with the Prophet) – who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If Allah intends to take the soul of a servant in a land, He makes him have a need in it."
"974","974/1283 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: That he blew his nose in his garment, then said: ""Bravo, bravo, Abu Huraira blows his nose in linen. I saw myself collapsing between the chamber of Aisha and the pulpit; people would say: 'He is mad,' but nothing was wrong with me except hunger."
"975","975/1284 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira: They said, ""O Messenger of Allah! We find in ourselves something that we do not like to speak of, and we would rather have anything than for it to be revealed to the sun."" He said, ""Have you truly found that?"" They said, ""Yes."" He said, ""That is clear faith."
"976","976/1286 (Sahih) From Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""People will not cease to ask about things that never happened, until they say: This is Allah, the Creator of everything, so who created Allah?!"
"977","977/1287 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Beware of suspicion; for suspicion is the most false of speech. Do not spy, do not compete, do not turn your backs on one another, do not envy one another, do not hate one another, and be—O servants of Allah—brothers."
"978","978/1288 (Sahih) From Anas who said: While the Prophet, peace be upon him, was with a woman from among his wives, a man passed by him. The Prophet, peace be upon him, called him and said: ""O so-and-so [إن] , this is my wife so-and-so!"" He said: I would not have suspected you of anything, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""Indeed, Satan flows through the son of Adam as blood flows."
"979","979/1289 (Sahih al-Isnad) from Abdullah (1) who said: ""The one from whom something is stolen continues to suspect (2) until he becomes greater than the thief."
"980","980/1292 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The natural disposition is five: circumcision, shaving the pubic hair(1), plucking the armpit hair, trimming the mustache, and clipping the nails."
"981","981/1297 (Sahih al-Isnad, Maqtu‘) from Ibrahim [هو ابن يزيد النخعي] who said: ""Our companions used to permit us all toys, except dogs."" Abu Abdullah said: He means for the boys."
"982","982/1300 (Hasan Sahih) From Abu Huraira who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saw a man chasing a pigeon, and said: ""A devil chasing his she-devil."
"983","983/1302 (Hasan al-Isnad) from Zayd ibn Thabit: That Umar ibn al-Khattab came to him one day seeking permission to enter, so he permitted him while his head was in the hand of a slave girl of his who was combing his hair, so he withdrew his head. Umar said: Let her comb your hair. He said: O Commander of the Faithful! If you had sent for me, I would have come to you. Umar said: ""The need is mine."
"984","984/1304 (Hasan al-isnad, Maqtu‘an) from Habib ibn Abi Thabit who said: ""They used to like that when a man narrated (hadith), he would not turn exclusively to one man, but rather include all of them."
"985","985/1305 (Hasan al-isnad, mawquf) from Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl who said: Abdullah [هو ابن مسعود] visited a man, and with him was a man from his companions. When they entered the house, his companion began to look around, so Abdullah said to him: ""By Allah, if your two eyes burst out, it would be better for you."
"986","986/1036 (Authentic chain) From Nafi‘: A group of people from Iraq entered upon Ibn ‘Umar, and they saw on a servant of his a collar of gold. Some of them looked at each other, so he said: ""How perceptive you are to evil!"
"987","987/1308 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The worst of my nation are the talkative, the boastful, the pompous, and the best of my nation are those with the best character."
"988","988/1310 (Hasan) From Ammar ibn Yasir who said: I heard the Prophet, peace be upon him, say: ""Whoever has two faces in this world will have two tongues of fire on the Day of Resurrection."" Then a man who was large passed by. He said: ""This one is among them."
"989","989/1311 (Sahih) From Aisha: A man sought permission to enter upon the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he said: ""Permit him, what a bad brother of his tribe he is."" When he entered, he spoke to him gently (and in another narration: he was cheerful with him/338). So I said: O Messenger of Allah! You said what you said, then you spoke gently to him? He said: ""O Aisha! The worst of people is the one whom people leave— or abandon— to avoid his foul language."" (And in a third narration: ""Indeed, Allah does not love the obscene, foul-mouthed one."")(1)."
"990","990/1312 (Sahih) From Imran ibn Husayn who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Modesty does not bring anything except good."" Then Bashir ibn Ka'b said: It is written in wisdom: ""Indeed, from modesty comes dignity, indeed, from modesty comes tranquility."" So Imran said to him: I narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah, and you narrate to me from your booklet?!"
"991","991/1313 From Ibn Umar(1) who said: ""Modesty and faith are paired together; if one of them is removed, the other is removed as well."
"992","992/1314 (Sahih) From Abu Bakra, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, [أنه] , who said: ""Modesty is part of faith, and faith is in Paradise, and foulness is from harshness, and harshness is in the Fire."
"993","993/1315 (Hasan) From Ali who said: ""The Prophet, peace be upon him, had a large head, great eyes, and when he walked, he leaned forward; as if he were walking on a slope, and when he turned, he turned completely."
"994","994/1317 (Sahih) From Abu Huraira; that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The strong one is not the one who overcomes others by wrestling, but the strong one is the one who controls himself when angry."
"995","995/1318 (Mawquf, its narrators are trustworthy, and it has been authentically reported as marfu‘) from Ibn Umar who said: ""There is no gulp greater in reward with Allah than a gulp of anger which a servant swallows, seeking the Face of Allah."
"996","996/1320 (Sahih li-ghayrih) from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Teach and make things easy, teach and make things easy—three times—and when you become angry, be silent (twice)."
"997","997/1321 (Hasan li-ghayrihi as mawquf and has been authentically reported as marfu‘) from ‘Ubayd al-Kindi who said: I heard ‘Ali say to Ibn al-Kawwa’: Do you know what the former said? ""Love your beloved moderately, perhaps he will become your enemy one day; and hate your enemy moderately, perhaps he will become your beloved one day."
"998","998/1322 (authentic chain) from Aslam, from Umar ibn al-Khattab who said: ""Let not your love be infatuation, nor your hatred destruction."" I said: How is that? He said: ""When you love, do not be infatuated like a child, and when you hate, do not wish destruction upon your companion."
"999","[تم بحمد الله] ."
